1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,304
A closed bottle of water never dries up.

2
00:00:03,304 --> 00:00:05,625
An open glass dries up in days.

3
00:00:06,025 --> 00:00:08,926
A soda bottle stays
calm until you open it.

4
00:00:08,926 --> 00:00:10,091
Then it fizzes.

5
00:00:10,541 --> 00:00:13,345
Diya drops ice into water in her kitchen.

6
00:00:13,345 --> 00:00:15,584
Ice and water sit side by side.

7
00:00:15,584 --> 00:00:17,288
Nothing seems to change.

8
00:00:17,638 --> 00:00:21,846
Last time we asked: at equilibrium,
does everything really stop?

9
00:00:22,346 --> 00:00:25,313
By the end, you will
see that nothing stops.

10
00:00:25,313 --> 00:00:28,328
Two changes run at the
same speed, both ways.

11
00:00:28,978 --> 00:00:30,801
Think of a shop with one door.

12
00:00:31,151 --> 00:00:33,398
Every minute, five people walk in.

13
00:00:33,398 --> 00:00:35,691
Every minute, five people walk out.

14
00:00:36,091 --> 00:00:38,249
The crowd inside stays the same.

15
00:00:38,249 --> 00:00:40,201
But the people keep changing.

16
00:00:40,601 --> 00:00:42,596
Not stopped, but balanced.

17
00:00:42,596 --> 00:00:44,592
This is dynamic equilibrium.

18
00:00:44,592 --> 00:00:46,587
Dynamic means always moving.

19
00:00:47,187 --> 00:00:51,751
Let's put ice and water on our molecule
bench, in a closed flask at zero

20
00:00:51,751 --> 00:00:52,912
degrees Celsius.

21
00:00:53,262 --> 00:00:56,402
Some molecules break free from
the ice and join the water.

22
00:00:56,402 --> 00:00:57,416
That is melting.

23
00:00:57,816 --> 00:01:01,381
At the same time, some water
molecules stick back onto the ice.

24
00:01:01,381 --> 00:01:02,462
That is freezing.

25
00:01:02,862 --> 00:01:03,762
Count them.

26
00:01:03,739 --> 00:01:05,541
Melting, five each second.

27
00:01:05,541 --> 00:01:07,390
Freezing, five each second.

28
00:01:07,390 --> 00:01:08,290
Equal.

29
00:01:08,372 --> 00:01:12,649
So the amount of ice stays the same,
and so does the amount of water.

30
00:01:13,049 --> 00:01:17,152
This happens at two seventy three
kelvin, at normal air pressure.

31
00:01:17,152 --> 00:01:19,329
That is the normal melting point.

32
00:01:19,929 --> 00:01:21,888
Next, water and its vapour.

33
00:01:21,888 --> 00:01:24,260
Vapour means the gas form of water.

34
00:01:24,610 --> 00:01:28,005
In a closed box, molecules
escape into the space above.

35
00:01:28,005 --> 00:01:29,318
That is evaporation.

36
00:01:29,718 --> 00:01:33,486
As vapour builds up, some molecules
hit the water and come back.

37
00:01:33,486 --> 00:01:34,792
That is condensation.

38
00:01:35,192 --> 00:01:36,967
Soon both speeds are equal.

39
00:01:36,967 --> 00:01:40,750
The level stops dropping, and
the vapour pressure stays fixed.

40
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:44,405
In an open glass, the
vapour drifts away for good.

41
00:01:44,405 --> 00:01:46,348
No balance, so it dries up.

42
00:01:46,748 --> 00:01:51,119
Water boils at one hundred degrees
Celsius at one point oh one three bar.

43
00:01:51,119 --> 00:01:53,203
That is its normal boiling point.

44
00:01:53,803 --> 00:01:56,045
Some solids turn straight into vapour.

45
00:01:56,045 --> 00:01:57,322
This is sublimation.

46
00:01:57,672 --> 00:02:00,809
Iodine, camphor and
ammonium chloride do this.

47
00:02:00,809 --> 00:02:04,180
In a closed jar, both
ways run at equal speeds.

48
00:02:04,930 --> 00:02:08,325
Now keep adding sugar to
water until no more dissolves.

49
00:02:08,325 --> 00:02:10,012
The solution is saturated.

50
00:02:10,762 --> 00:02:12,314
Has dissolving stopped?

51
00:02:12,314 --> 00:02:16,852
Scientists added sugar with a
radioactive tag, a marker they can track.

52
00:02:17,602 --> 00:02:21,047
The tag soon showed up in the
solution and in the solid.

53
00:02:21,047 --> 00:02:23,387
Sugar keeps dissolving and coming back.

54
00:02:24,137 --> 00:02:25,472
Now gases in liquids.

55
00:02:25,472 --> 00:02:27,666
Why does soda fizz when you open it?

56
00:02:28,016 --> 00:02:32,480
In a sealed bottle, carbon dioxide
sits above the drink at high pressure.

57
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:34,843
Gas goes in and out at equal speeds.

58
00:02:35,243 --> 00:02:38,959
Henry's law: the mass of gas that
dissolves is proportional to the gas

59
00:02:38,959 --> 00:02:40,477
pressure above the liquid.

60
00:02:40,477 --> 00:02:42,606
Double the pressure, double the gas.

61
00:02:43,006 --> 00:02:44,074
Open the cap.

62
00:02:44,074 --> 00:02:47,379
The pressure falls, so
the extra gas bubbles out.

63
00:02:47,379 --> 00:02:48,279
Fizz.

64
00:02:48,333 --> 00:02:51,547
Warm the drink, and even
less gas stays dissolved.

65
00:02:51,547 --> 00:02:53,348
Warm soda goes flat faster.

66
00:02:53,948 --> 00:02:54,848
Compare.

67
00:02:54,633 --> 00:02:58,493
A closed bottle: molecules escape
and return at equal speeds.

68
00:02:58,493 --> 00:03:01,416
An open glass: they
escape and never return.

69
00:03:01,816 --> 00:03:03,007
Pause and think.

70
00:03:03,007 --> 00:03:06,192
Can an open glass of water
ever reach equilibrium?

71
00:03:09,392 --> 00:03:10,292
No.

72
00:03:09,855 --> 00:03:13,982
Equilibrium needs a closed system,
so the vapour cannot escape.

73
00:03:14,632 --> 00:03:18,067
Now the rules every physical
equilibrium follows.

74
00:03:18,417 --> 00:03:19,317
One.

75
00:03:18,909 --> 00:03:22,731
It happens only in a closed
system, at a fixed temperature.

76
00:03:23,131 --> 00:03:24,031
Two.

77
00:03:23,592 --> 00:03:26,384
Both opposite changes
go on at the same speed.

78
00:03:26,784 --> 00:03:27,684
Three.

79
00:03:27,348 --> 00:03:31,227
Things we can measure, like
pressure and amount, stay constant.

80
00:03:31,627 --> 00:03:32,527
Four.

81
00:03:32,155 --> 00:03:36,714
Each has one fixed value at a given
temperature, like the vapour pressure.

82
00:03:37,114 --> 00:03:38,014
Five.

83
00:03:37,614 --> 00:03:41,207
The size of that value tells
us how far the change has gone.

84
00:03:41,807 --> 00:03:42,831
Remember this.

85
00:03:42,831 --> 00:03:44,983
Equilibrium does not mean stopped.

86
00:03:44,983 --> 00:03:46,255
It means balanced.

87
00:03:46,955 --> 00:03:48,645
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:48,945 --> 00:03:53,471
A drink holds one point five grams of
carbon dioxide per litre at one bar.

89
00:03:53,471 --> 00:03:56,690
At the same temperature,
it is sealed at four bar.

90
00:03:56,690 --> 00:03:57,977
How much dissolves?

91
00:03:58,477 --> 00:03:59,377
Henry's law.

92
00:03:59,137 --> 00:04:01,488
The mass dissolved is
proportional to the pressure.

93
00:04:02,238 --> 00:04:04,644
The pressure became four times bigger.

94
00:04:04,644 --> 00:04:07,068
Four bar divided by one bar is four.

95
00:04:07,818 --> 00:04:10,656
So the mass is four times one point five.

96
00:04:10,656 --> 00:04:12,144
Six grams per litre.

97
00:04:12,894 --> 00:04:15,399
Open the cap, back to about one bar.

98
00:04:15,399 --> 00:04:17,703
Only one point five grams can stay.

99
00:04:17,703 --> 00:04:19,817
Four and a half grams fizz out.

100
00:04:20,467 --> 00:04:21,931
Now, the exam trap.

101
00:04:22,281 --> 00:04:25,628
Students write: at equilibrium,
melting and freezing have stopped.

102
00:04:26,028 --> 00:04:26,928
Wrong.

103
00:04:26,629 --> 00:04:28,705
Both go on, at equal speeds.

104
00:04:28,705 --> 00:04:30,880
Only the overall change is zero.

105
00:04:31,530 --> 00:04:32,739
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:32,739 --> 00:04:35,361
Boards ask for the
characteristics and Henry's law.

107
00:04:35,361 --> 00:04:37,650
Entrance tests ask
which statement is true.

108
00:04:38,250 --> 00:04:39,442
Exam level now.

109
00:04:39,442 --> 00:04:43,342
Water and its vapour are at
equilibrium in a closed vessel.

110
00:04:43,342 --> 00:04:45,201
Which statement is correct?

111
00:04:45,201 --> 00:04:46,444
Pause and solve.

112
00:04:51,844 --> 00:04:53,142
The answer is B.

113
00:04:53,142 --> 00:04:56,857
The vapour pressure stays
constant at a given temperature.

114
00:04:57,357 --> 00:05:01,452
Evaporation and condensation
both go on at equal speeds.

115
00:05:01,452 --> 00:05:02,809
So A is wrong.

116
00:05:03,309 --> 00:05:07,828
Vapour pressure depends on the temperature,
not on how much water is there.

117
00:05:07,828 --> 00:05:08,929
So C is wrong.

118
00:05:09,429 --> 00:05:12,224
An open vessel lets the vapour escape.

119
00:05:12,224 --> 00:05:13,517
So D is wrong.

120
00:05:14,117 --> 00:05:15,658
Here is your recap.

121
00:05:15,658 --> 00:05:17,242
Screenshot this card.

122
00:05:20,442 --> 00:05:22,628
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

123
00:05:23,028 --> 00:05:23,928
One.

124
00:05:23,486 --> 00:05:25,549
What does dynamic equilibrium mean?

125
00:05:26,349 --> 00:05:27,249
Two.

126
00:05:26,827 --> 00:05:30,204
Why can an open glass of
water never reach equilibrium?

127
00:05:31,004 --> 00:05:31,904
Three.

128
00:05:31,463 --> 00:05:33,713
Why does soda fizz when
you open the bottle?

129
00:05:34,613 --> 00:05:38,623
Next time: does a chemical reaction
also keep running both ways?

130
00:05:38,623 --> 00:05:41,652
We find out in Equilibrium
in Chemical Processes.
